{"count": 26451, "next": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=976", "previous": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=974", "results": [{"id": 75680, "name": "Bernard Herrmann", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2138499, "title": "Citizen Kane (TEFC)", "work_id": 75988, "role": "music", "role_id": 949, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2138486, "title": "Citizen Kane", "work_id": 79537, "role": "music", "role_id": 949, "is_primary": false}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "1911-06-29", "date_of_death": "1975-12-24", "genders": [{"id": 9, "name": "Male", "slug": "male"}], "languages": [{"id": 1, "name": "English", "slug": "english"}], "places_of_operation": [{"id": 1, "name": "United States of America", "slug": "united-states-of-america"}], "biography": "Bernard Herrmann (born Maximillian Herman; June 29, 1911 – December 24, 1975) was an American composer and conductor best known for his work in composing for films. As a conductor, he championed the music of lesser-known composers. He is widely regarded as one of the greatest film composers. Alex Ross writes that \"Over four decades, he revolutionized movie scoring by abandoning the illustrative musical techniques that dominated Hollywood in the 1930s and imposing his own peculiar harmonic and rhythmic vocabulary.\"An Academy Award-winner for The Devil and Daniel Webster (1941), Herrmann is known for his collaborations with Alfred Hitchcock, notably The Man Who Knew Too Much (1956) (where he makes a cameo as the conductor at Royal Albert Hall), Vertigo (1958), North by Northwest (1959), Psycho (1960), The Birds (1963) (as \"sound consultant\") and Marnie (1964). He worked in radio drama, composing for Orson Welles's The Mercury Theater on the Air, and his first film score was for Welles's film debut, Citizen Kane (1941). His other credits include Jane Eyre (1943), Anna and the King of Siam (1946), The Ghost and Mrs. Muir (1947), The Day the Earth Stood Still (1951), Cape Fear (1962), Fahrenheit 451 (1966) and Twisted Nerve (1968). Herrmann scored films that were inspired by Hitchcock, like François Truffaut's The Bride Wore Black (1968) and Brian De Palma's Sisters (1972) and Obsession (1976). He composed the scores for several fantasy films by Ray Harryhausen, and composed for television, including Have Gun – Will Travel and Rod Serling's The Twilight Zone. His last score, recorded shortly before his death, was for Martin Scorsese's Taxi Driver (1976).", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T14:11:11.981451+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 3634, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernard_Herrmann"}], "uuid": "7e53d532-e810-473b-b8d0-901e544f4984", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "34044", "external_references": [{"id": 25166, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q296804"}, {"id": 27472, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "12491561"}, {"id": 27473, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n81035338"}, {"id": 27474, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n81035338"}], "date_modified": "2023-09-05T17:59:12.341284+10:00"}, {"id": 75679, "name": "Gregg Toland", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2475694, "title": "Wuthering Heights", "work_id": 80109, "role": "cinematographer", "role_id": 32, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 1988471, "title": "The Little foxes", "work_id": 84620, "role": "cinematographer", "role_id": 32, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2138495, "title": "Citizen Kane (TEFC)", "work_id": 75988, "role": "cinematographer", "role_id": 32, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2138481, "title": "Citizen Kane", "work_id": 79537, "role": "cinematographer", "role_id": 32, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2293111, "title": "These three", "work_id": 85357, "role": "cinematographer", "role_id": 32, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2351958, "title": "The Grapes of Wrath", "work_id": 80788, "role": "cinematographer", "role_id": 32, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2107178, "title": "Ball of fire", "work_id": 84667, "role": "cinematographer", "role_id": 32, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2305563, "title": "Song of the south", "work_id": 89350, "role": "cinematographer", "role_id": 32, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 802949, "title": "Nana", "work_id": 89523, "role": "cinematographer", "role_id": 32, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2475883, "title": "Intermezzo", "work_id": 85624, "role": "cinematographer", "role_id": 32, "is_primary": false}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "1904-05-29", "date_of_death": "1948-09-28", "genders": [{"id": 9, "name": "Male", "slug": "male"}], "languages": [], "places_of_operation": [{"id": 1, "name": "United States of America", "slug": "united-states-of-america"}], "biography": "Gregg Wesley Toland, A.S.C. (May 29, 1904 – September 28, 1948) was an American cinematographer known for his innovative use of techniques such as deep focus, examples of which can be found in his work on Orson Welles' Citizen Kane (1941), William Wyler's The Best Years of Our Lives (1946), and John Ford's The Grapes of Wrath, and The Long Voyage Home (both, 1940). He is also known for his work as a director of photography for Wuthering Heights (1939), The Westerner (1940), Ball of Fire (1941), The Outlaw (1943), Song of the South (1946) and The Bishop's Wife (1947).\nToland earned six Academy Award nominations for Best Cinematography, and won for his work on Wuthering Heights. He was voted one of the top 10 most influential cinematographers in the history of film by the International Cinematographers Guild in 2003.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-09-05T20:22:37.880278+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 4107, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregg_Toland"}], "uuid": "dbd92543-efa8-4131-bd8d-c696473dab6a", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "38145", "external_references": [{"id": 25144, "source": {"name": "